WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:04.420
Ever wonder just what kind of weird stuff
is buried in the Netflix catalog?

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.980
You could spend all day scrolling around in
search of the strange, but we've got you covered.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.650
Let's take a look at some of the most bizarre
films you can find on Netflix.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.730
Wetlands

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.010
Based on the novel bya Charlotte Roche, this
movie features unrealistically massive amounts

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.000
of bodily fluids to tell the stark, true,
and riveting story of an 18-year-old woman

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
coming of age.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.660
While exploring the human body in an act of
rebellion against her mother, she finds herself

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.250
trying to rekindle a love between her divorced
parents while falling for her male nurse — a

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.219
man she met after a shaving accident gone
terribly wrong.

00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:37.800
Hard to be a God

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.420
The Guardian called Hard to be a God "a monumental,
and monumentally mad film."

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.850
Based on the novel of the same name by Arkady
and Boris Strugatsky, it's set in what looks

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:51.089
like a medieval sci-fi Russia — but on a
different planet that looks remarkably like

00:00:51.089 --> 00:00:52.089
Earth.

00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:54.960
There, two competing factions called "Blacks"
and "Greys" are at war.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.500
The peasants are disfigured, ugly and stuck
in copious amounts of blood and mud for eternity.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.860
The film forces its audience to ponder another
world, like ours, where life is, and has been,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.420
much worse.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.420
Upstream Color

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.500
This is a movie about a parasite that passes
between humans, pigs, and orchids.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.719
Surprisingly, that's not the weirdest thing
about it.

00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:19.270
A man known as "The Thief" manages to implant
a worm in a woman called Kris that allows

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.500
her to mentally communicate with pigs — which
also allows her to come to grips with her

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.570
very dysfunctional relationship with her boyfriend
Jeff.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:30.899
It prompted Slate critic Forrest Wickman to
write, "I just saw Upstream Color … What

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:32.009
just happened?"

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:34.220
A question we're still trying to answer.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.740
God of Cookery

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:40.070
This film about the rise, fall, and rise again
of a Chinese chef tickles your funny bone

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:42.650
while playing to a very specific audience.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:47.270
Variety describes it as "an entertaining,
often very funny blend of high-energy Cantonese

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.290
comedy and culinary kung fu."

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:53.170
But if you're not a fan of both cooking and
martial arts, don't worry, because it's hilarious

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:55.810
and daring enough for any movie lover to enjoy.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.330
3 Headed Shark Attack

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:01.610
If you looked at the title of this movie and
thought it screamed for actor Danny Trejo,

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:05.140
then you're in luck: you'll find him in a
fishing boat full of guns and ammo, beheading

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:06.460
a three-headed shark.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.121
The plot is straightforward enough: the mutant
creature in the title attacks a ship and it

00:02:10.121 --> 00:02:13.720
won't stop until it's eaten everyone, while
the passengers use anything they can to fight

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.320
it off.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.860
What's not to love?

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:18.920
"Oh, shoot!"

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.140
It's like Jaws, plus two more heads, and also
terrible.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.460
But in the very best way.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.190
Now what could possibly top that?

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.720
What about a movie called...

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.470
The ABCs of Death

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.460
What if you hired 26 directors­, one for
each letter of the alphabet?

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:37.080
Then what if you tasked each of them with
crafting a short film that needed only to

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.820
fall into a certain runtime and follow a narrative
built around death?

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.550
That's exactly what producers Ant Thompson
and Tim League did with this experimental

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.120
anthology film.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.080
Watch it for the cinematography — and for
some seriously boundary pushing butt jokes.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.530
He Never Died

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.790
The tagline to He Never Died gets right to
the point: "It's hard to live when you can't

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.790
die."

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.230
Actor, singer, author and bicycle shorts enthusiast
Henry Rollins plays Jack.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.950
Jack's a kind of mysterious anti-hero whose
listless drift through middle age is upended

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:07.720
when his teenage daughter is kidnapped.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:12.210
Rollins plays Jack as a subtle homage to Arnold
Schwarzenegger's Terminator, which is probably

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.860
the least weird thing about the film.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.500
"Probably just gonna go kill another room
full of people."

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.100
" You are?!"

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.100
"Probably…"

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.500
"Come on!!"

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.650
Daemonium

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.430
Unless it's coming from Guillermo del Toro,
a little bit of fantasy horror goes a long

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:26.430
way.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.780
Where del Toro enthralled audiences with Pan's
Labyrinth, Argentinian director Pablo Pares'

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:36.090
Daemonium opts to go full-out weird at the
expense of plot, pacing, and character development.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.600
That's cool, though, because instead Daemonium
delivers a bizarre mix of demons, wizards,

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.320
and martial arts action that flirts with your
eyes like a splash of drain cleaner.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.900
Just about every ten minutes, Daemonium goes
from bad to badass to bad acid and back again,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.560
an endless carousel completely unfettered
by logic.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.720
Just sit back and enjoy the ride.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.090
The Scribbler

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.070
What if you were a killer and didn't know
it?

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.570
That's the question asked by The Scribbler,
which follows a woman named Suki with multiple

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:12.320
personalities living in an apartment building
sanctioned for psychiatric patients.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.160
One by one, the women in the building start
dying, allegedly jumping to their deaths.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.760
The movie is told within the framework of
Suki's interrogation by the police after the

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.540
fact, giving it a weird sense of altered chronology
and an unreliable narrative — after all,

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.180
it's a story being told by a crazy woman.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.930
So yeah, it gets way weird as the story progresses.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:33.870
Superpowers, insanity, a totally freaky God
machine — all hidden behind the closed doors

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.350
of a psychiatric institute.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:39.370
It boasts some pretty sleek visuals and a
unique story line, if nothing else.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.130
Night Watch/Day Watch

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:46.290
Before he made his English-language debut
with 2008's Wanted or his follow-up, 2012's

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:50.510
Abraham Lincoln: Vampire Hunter, Timur Bekmambetov
wrote and directed the double feature of Night

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.070
Watch and Day Watch.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:55.500
These two sci-fi films simply don't follow
any of the norms of Hollywood blockbusters

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.240
— and that's a good thing.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.240
Probably.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.710
The dreamlike dissonance of these movies is
enough to make you question your own sanity,

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.320
since the only constant rule in the Day/Night
Watch universe is "make it shiny."

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.530
All the plot threads about alternate dimensions
and vampires and ancient forces do come together

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:13.090
into a sort of coherent story, but it's a
hard weave to figure out.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.340
Good luck!

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.340
Deathgasm

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.170
Once upon a time, someone watched Scott Pilgrim
vs. the World and thought, "This would be

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:24.510
a lot better with more heavy metal and naked
women, and maybe someone, like, beats up a

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:25.630
demon with a sex toy."

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.430
It's a thought we've all had at some point.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.010
You're having that thought right now.

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.170
Admit it.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:35.020
Anyway, whatever caused it to happen, Deathgasm
is now an irretrievable piece of the world.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.250
It's out there, unable to be undone.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.100
So what is the film supposedly about?

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.300
Well, it follows four teenagers who start
a metal band in their garage and inadvertently

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.050
play a song that unleashes hell on their quiet
suburban neighborhood.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:49.000
To save the town, they have to pretty much
kill everyone in it.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.070
New Zealand's been giving us some great quirky
comedies lately, like What We Do In The Shadows,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.980
and Deathgasm is certainly part of the gang.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.190
Even if it's the weird kid that hangs out
in the back acting like a spaz.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.190
Zombeavers

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:04.560
A movie called Zombeavers featuring singer
John Mayer and comedian Bill Burr should get

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:05.560
your attention.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:09.000
"Hey man, you see that deer up there?"

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.000
"Yeah. I see it."

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.080
"I don't think you do…"

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.380
Especially when the plot follows the fallout
from a toxic waste spill that sends a swamp

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:21.700
full of zombie beavers to attack a gang of
helpless teenagers.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:26.700
According to RogerEbert.com, "Zombeavers is
exactly what it sounds like: A stoner-friendly

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:29.310
horror-comedy about undead beavers."

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.320
Finally, truth in advertising.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.600
Thanks for watching!

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.230
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.360
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

